[1] The [28th Virginia Infantry Regiment](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_Infantry_Regiment/fRjg0tWr) battle flag, a [Confederate battle flag](https://reference.org/facts/Confederate_battle_flag/eAywzjJj), was captured by Private [Marshall Sherman](https://reference.org/facts/Marshall_Sherman/NUH6TMn2) of the [1st Minnesota Infantry Regiment](https://reference.org/facts/1st_Minnesota_Infantry_Regiment/J8jrm26b) at the [Battle of Gettysburg](https://reference.org/facts/Battle_of_Gettysburg/wx9evLcK). The flag was brought to [Minnesota](https://reference.org/facts/Minnesota/ncgVuC4Q), displayed at [the state's capitol](https://reference.org/facts/Minnesota_State_Capitol/H0t87Fqm), and later placed in the permanent collection of the [Minnesota Historical Society](https://reference.org/facts/Minnesota_Historical_Society/FEVWrvQp). Despite repeated requests from [Virginia](https://reference.org/facts/Virginia/WSyGxzaH) dating back to 1960 and a 1905 [congressional resolution](<https://reference.org/facts/Resolution_(law)/o4nlnvCX>) urging return of Civil War flags, Minnesota has declined to return it. The flag remains mostly intact, though with some damage, and its exact location is undisclosed for security reasons.

[2] Background

[3] During the [American Civil War](https://reference.org/facts/American_Civil_War/A9FT3ksD), flags played important strategic and emotional roles in battles. They were used to guide soldiers and mark advances. Enemy forces would concentrate fire on [standard-bearers](https://reference.org/facts/Standard-bearer/kXk1jd1t), and if they were hit, soldiers near them would pick up the flags. As a result, flag designs were created to be recognizable and effective on the battlefield. One design developed on the Confederate side was known as the "southern cross", consisting of a red flag with a diagonal blue St. Andrew's cross (a symbol associated with the Scottish/Celtic culture, which had a strong tradition in the region) and white stars. This was carried by the [Army of Northern Virginia](https://reference.org/facts/Army_of_Northern_Virginia/UULyjGEp), with nine different variations; this design was used for the 28th Virginia battle flag.[2](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:2)

[4] Creation

[5] The 28th Virginia battle flag is made from wool [bunting](<https://reference.org/facts/Bunting_(textile)/plGEUTuT>) that originated in the [British Isles](https://reference.org/facts/British_Isles/4fBquvMU). It was likely sewn by a Southern woman at her home using a "flag kit" produced by the [quartermaster](https://reference.org/facts/Quartermaster/Porth958) of the Army of Northern Virginia; this work may have been motivated by profit or patriotism. The sewn-on white stars and edging may have been made from cotton fabric, which was turned a brownish color by the [acidic](https://reference.org/facts/Acid/FySU18n1) conditions on Civil War battlefields that resulted from the use of [black powder](https://reference.org/facts/Black_powder/AEcB6HCR).[3](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:3)

[6] Appearance and issue

[7] The 28th Virginia battle flag, as an instance of the typical Army of Northern Virginia design, is a square red flag bearing a blue [saltire](https://reference.org/facts/Saltire/5kG0mdDF) with white stars.[4](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:4) The flag measures 44.5 inches (1,130 mm) in height and 48 inches (1,200 mm) in width.[5](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:5) Records suggest that the battle flag was issued to [George Pickett](https://reference.org/facts/George_Pickett/koT9Fjuo)'s division of the Army of Northern Virginia within a few weeks prior to the [Battle of Gettysburg](https://reference.org/facts/Battle_of_Gettysburg/wx9evLcK), when it would be captured. The battle would be its first use in combat.[6](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:6)

[8] Capture

[9] See also: [Pickett's Charge](https://reference.org/facts/Pickett%2527s_Charge/U1TU6KNH) and [high-water mark of the Confederacy](https://reference.org/facts/High-water_mark_of_the_Confederacy/xmHl1FM0)

[10] On the morning of July 3, 1863, Confederate General [Robert E. Lee](https://reference.org/facts/Robert_E._Lee/6ooHzprY) ordered [an attack](https://reference.org/facts/Pickett%2527s_Charge/U1TU6KNH) on the [Union Army](https://reference.org/facts/Union_Army/WaDC9c06) during the [Battle of Gettysburg](https://reference.org/facts/Battle_of_Gettysburg/wx9evLcK). The 28th Virginia Infantry Regiment was part of a [brigade](https://reference.org/facts/Brigade/UEvdyao1) led by Brigadier General [Richard Garnett](https://reference.org/facts/Richard_B._Garnett/TyeUF8Td), positioned at the point of a lopsided V-shape formed by the marching Confederate troops. The Union soldiers, located ahead of and above the Confederate troops, opened fire, but the Confederates broke through up [Cemetery Ridge](https://reference.org/facts/Cemetery_Ridge/0q7rInem) in places, reaching the area that would become known as the [high-water mark of the Confederacy](https://reference.org/facts/High-water_mark_of_the_Confederacy/xmHl1FM0). Confederate reinforcements did not arrive, while Union troops entered the breaches.[7](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:7)

[11] The Union's [1st Minnesota Infantry Regiment](https://reference.org/facts/1st_Minnesota_Infantry_Regiment/J8jrm26b) was ordered to [attack the flank](https://reference.org/facts/Flanking_maneuver/QpkIXjLe) of the Confederate troops, and did so while protecting their own flag after the last remaining member of their [color guard](https://reference.org/facts/Colour_guard/YhVWvBnb) was shot through the hand.[8](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:8) More than 70% of the regiment's members were killed, wounded, or captured in the course of the battle.[9](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:9): 142

[12] During this attack by the 1st Minnesota, Sergeant John Eakin of the 28th Virginia was shot three times while carrying the 28th Virginia battle flag. A [private](<https://reference.org/facts/Private_(rank)/GEH28Sb2>) was immediately shot upon picking up the battle flag, after which Colonel Robert Allen picked it up and was also immediately fatally wounded. Allen handed the flag to Lieutenant John Lee, who stepped on top of the Union wall and began waving it. The pole of the flag was shot, but Lee picked the flag back up and continued to wave it even after being wounded.[10](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:10)

[13] The 28th Virginia battle flag was subsequently captured by Private [Marshall Sherman](https://reference.org/facts/Marshall_Sherman/NUH6TMn2) of the 1st Minnesota Infantry Regiment, Company C. Sherman would later be awarded the [Medal of Honor](https://reference.org/facts/Medal_of_Honor/aCKrPP3N) for his actions during the battle.[11](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:11) Accounts of the capture vary:[12](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:12)

[14] Sherman's account

[15] After the Battle of Gettysburg, Marshall Sherman told a [newspaper reporter](https://reference.org/facts/Journalist/LADe3dzs) that he had run directly toward Confederate Lieutenant John Lee, eventually holding his [bayonet](https://reference.org/facts/Bayonet/YMU1F4IT) inches from Lee's chest and saying "Throw down that flag or I'll run you through". In Sherman's account, Lee hesitated, Sherman shouted again, and Lee dropped the flag and put his hands up, after which Sherman picked up the flag and took Lee as a prisoner.[13](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:13)

[16] Daniel Bond's account

[17] An account by Daniel Bond, a private in the 1st Minnesota Infantry Regiment along with Sherman, conflicts with Sherman's account. According to Bond, the flag was sighted resting unguarded against a tree, and Sherman obtained it by running to it faster than Bond could.[14](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:14)

[18] Subsequent possession

[19] In October 1864, the [United States War Department](https://reference.org/facts/United_States_Department_of_War/ar4kWMdI) ruled that all captured Confederate flags "belong to the United States" and were required to be deposited with the department for an inventory. The number 58 was stenciled on the 28th Virginia battle flag in this inventory, indicating that Sherman deposited it with the department at some point prior to the official inventory in 1867.[15](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:15)

[20] Possession disputes

[21] 19th century

[22] In 1887, a group of American Civil War [veterans](https://reference.org/facts/Veteran/u72hVs9M) from [Pennsylvania](https://reference.org/facts/Pennsylvania/HGqC5bBm) planning a reunion at Gettysburg proposed the return of Confederate battle flags from three units, including the 28th Virginia battle flag, in hopes of enticing Confederate veterans to attend. [Grover Cleveland](https://reference.org/facts/Grover_Cleveland/4TPPTvRN), then president of the United States, issued an [executive order](https://reference.org/facts/Executive_order/JwJJXBar) in support of the plan. However, many Union veterans opposed the idea, as did some Southerners. Opposers included then-[Governor of Virginia](https://reference.org/facts/Governor_of_Virginia/MbRx2GMT) [Fitzhugh Lee](https://reference.org/facts/Fitzhugh_Lee/jkjedvSJ) (a Confederate veteran and the nephew of Robert E. Lee) as well as [Jefferson Davis](https://reference.org/facts/Jefferson_Davis/CK3RQyI0) who stated that the flags belonged to the capturing states and that returning them would break "all known military precedents". Cleveland eventually rescinded his executive order; the event was successfully held without the return of flags, drawing 500 Pennsylvanian and 200 Confederate veterans.[16](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:16)

[23] In an 1888 inventory by the War Department, the flag was "supposed to have been loaned and never returned". One hypothesis suggests that Sherman kept the flag, though this does not explain how it was inventoried at the War Department in 1867. [Alexander Ramsey](https://reference.org/facts/Alexander_Ramsey/PIArWnxh) may have retrieved it from the department while serving as its secretary between 1879 and 1881, and subsequently taken it to Minnesota where he became the first president of the [Minnesota Historical Society](https://reference.org/facts/Minnesota_Historical_Society/FEVWrvQp). After being returned to Minnesota, the flag was periodically exhibited at the [Minnesota State Capitol](https://reference.org/facts/Minnesota_State_Capitol/H0t87Fqm) for several years. It was displayed prominently at Sherman's 1896 funeral, but because it was not mentioned in his [will](https://reference.org/facts/Will_and_testament/PoRXxpEM), it eventually became a part of the permanent collection at the Minnesota Historical Society.[17](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:17)

[24] 20th century

[25] In 1905, the [United States Congress](https://reference.org/facts/United_States_Congress/mJlU46K7) passed a resolution directing that captured flags from the American Civil War should be returned to the places from which they originated.[18](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:18) The resolution was intended to celebrate the cooperation between the Northern and Southern states in the [Spanish-American War](https://reference.org/facts/Spanish-American_War/ScKbBPAv). However, the 28th Virginia battle flag could not be returned, as it was not in the possession of the War Department.[19](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:19)

[26] As early as 1960, a movement in Virginia called for the "recapture" of the 28th Virginia battle flag, wanting it to be returned from Minnesota to Virginia.[20](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:20) In 1961, Virginia requested the return of the flag in commemoration of the 100th anniversary of the Civil War, but it was not returned.[21](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:21) The request was made by John Jennings, then director of the [Virginia Historical Society](https://reference.org/facts/Virginia_Historical_Society/eGe4m5Sz), after the Minnesota Historical Society had returned a flag that had been captured from a Georgia Confederate regiment by the [2nd Minnesota Infantry Regiment](https://reference.org/facts/2nd_Minnesota_Infantry_Regiment/fl2T2kaw). In a letter to Jennings, the director of the Minnesota society stated that the actions of the 1st Minnesota Infantry Regiment had been one of the proudest moments in the history of the state, and argued that the flag "has greater historical value if it remains in Minnesota than if it is returned to Virginia."[22](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:22)

[27] In 1998, [Civil War reenactors](https://reference.org/facts/Civil_War_reenactor/sJKEwph4) in Virginia requested that the flag be returned[23](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:23) prior to the 135th anniversary of the Battle of Gettysburg.[24](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:24) The group was led by Chris Caveness, an insurance worker in [Roanoke, Virginia](https://reference.org/facts/Roanoke%2c_Virginia/ndubIcER) who had found the 1905 congressional resolution and believed that it provided a legal basis for the request.[25](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:25) However, the flag was not returned.[26](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:26) [Skip Humphrey](https://reference.org/facts/Skip_Humphrey/wEwD6trp), then [Attorney General of Minnesota](https://reference.org/facts/Attorney_General_of_Minnesota/ppQYgj9Q), stated that Virginia had no legal right to the flag despite the 1905 congressional resolution; he additionally noted that the flag could not be considered stolen because of the six-year [statute of limitations](https://reference.org/facts/Statute_of_limitations/lRTc8Qmb) in Minnesota. Caveness subsequently sought a loan of the flag to the [Salem Museum](https://reference.org/facts/Williams%25E2%2580%2593Brown_House_and_Store/G7pyPsdW), but was unsuccessful.[27](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:27)

[28] In 2000, members of the [Virginia General Assembly](https://reference.org/facts/Virginia_General_Assembly/w2qPY4c7) requested the return of the flag to Virginia, but it was not returned.[28](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:28) The group of [Virginia State Senators](https://reference.org/facts/Senate_of_Virginia/vrrQ0AhB) from the southwestern portion of the state were inspired to make the request by Caveness's Civil War reenactment group and their efforts to have the flag returned. They created a resolution and brought it to the floor of the Virginia Senate. One of the resolution's sponsors, [John S. Edwards](<https://reference.org/facts/John_S._Edwards_(Virginia_politician)/BmDg6tGF>), described the proposed return of the flag as "a matter of state pride" and stated that he didn't know why Minnesota needed it; Ian Stewart, deputy director of the Minnesota Historical Society, responded by disagreeing with the premise that the flag was more important to Virginia than Minnesota and stated that "Unless there's a compelling legal reason to return it, we are not inclined to do so." Stewart additionally questioned why the Virginia Senate was raising an issue about a Confederate symbol, noting the then-ongoing controversy over the display of a Confederate flag in South Carolina.[29](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:29) The Virginia Senate approved the resolution.[30](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:30)

[29] In response to the 2000 resolution by Virginia, Minnesota Governor [Jesse Ventura](https://reference.org/facts/Jesse_Ventura/0WrMuxCo) stated "Why? I mean, we won," and that "We took it, that makes it our heritage."[31](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:31)[32](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:32)

[30] 21st century

[31] In 2002, [John S. Brown](<https://reference.org/facts/John_S._Brown_(general)/3u4ubEos>), then Chief of Military History at the [United States Army Center of Military History](https://reference.org/facts/United_States_Army_Center_of_Military_History/f0THjDEo), declared that the flag should be housed in a military history museum in Virginia.

[32] In 2003, officials in Virginia including then-Governor of Virginia [Mark Warner](https://reference.org/facts/Mark_Warner/xp35M2JJ) demanded the return of the flag; Minnesota Governor [Tim Pawlenty](https://reference.org/facts/Tim_Pawlenty/SJL17b8q) responded that Virginia was "not getting it. [...] We believe it's rightfully ours, and we're not giving it back to Virginia."

[33] In 2013, 150 years after the Battle of Gettysburg, the governor of Virginia requested to borrow the flag. Minnesota Governor [Mark Dayton](https://reference.org/facts/Mark_Dayton/Bpux6wS2) declined.[33](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:33)

[34] Present condition

[35] Based on photos of the 28th Virginia battle flag, less than one percent of its fabric is missing, according to Fonda Thomsen of Textile Preservation Associates, Inc. The middle [eyelet](https://reference.org/facts/Grommet/aCzFKxbR) is torn, while there are roughly 12 holes in the red quadrants, and some holes in the border of the fly end. The flag has not been tested for blood,[34](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:34) though some reports have indicated that it is bloodstained.[35](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:35) It is kept in a drawer inside a cabinet within the Minnesota Historical Society, the location of which is undisclosed for security reasons.[36](https://reference.org/facts/28th_Virginia_battle_flag/T0Da0b4P#fn:36)

